---
import ContentLayout from '../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const asideLinks = [
  { label: 'Why this site exists', href: '#why-this-site-exists' },
  { label: 'Independence', href: '#independence' },
  { label: 'How content is maintained', href: '#how-content-is-maintained' },
  { label: 'How the site is funded', href: '#how-the-site-is-funded' },
  { label: 'Contact', href: '#contact' },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="About FixProblemNow"
  metaTitle="About FixProblemNow: Independent Tech Help"
  description="FixProblemNow is an independent troubleshooting library. Learn what the site covers, how it stays independent, and how to report a problem with a guide."
  canonical="/about/"
  eyebrow="About"
  intro="FixProblemNow is an independent reference site for everyday technology problems. It exists to give clear, safety-aware steps without remote access, accounts, or manufactured urgency."
  asideLinks={asideLinks}
  asideNote="The site is not affiliated with any manufacturer, software vendor, or repair company."
>
  <h2 id="why-this-site-exists">Why this site exists</h2>
  <p>
    Technology help is often scattered across forum threads, video tutorials, and support pages
    that assume you already know the right terms. A search for a simple symptom can return a dozen
    contradictory fixes, several download links of unknown origin, and advice that starts with a
    factory reset. That is a poor starting point when the device holds work, photos, messages, or
    account access.
  </p>
  <p>
    {site.name} takes a different approach. Each guide starts from a symptom you can actually see,
    explains the likely causes in plain language, and orders the checks from least to most
    disruptive. Risk, preparation, verification, and a stopping point are part of the page rather
    than an afterthought. Where a manufacturer’s own tool is required, the guide sends you to the
    manufacturer instead of offering an unofficial substitute.
  </p>

  <h2 id="independence">Independence</h2>
  <p>
    {site.name} is not affiliated with Microsoft, Apple, Google, Samsung, Dell, HP, Lenovo, Canon,
    Epson, Brother, Amazon, Roku, or any other device maker, software vendor, internet provider, or
    repair service. Brand and product names are used only to describe compatibility, menus, or
    official support paths.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site does not provide remote support, sell repairs, sell parts, or act as an intermediary
    for account recovery. There is no account system, no device scan, and no “live technician”
    chat. If another site claims to be us while asking for remote access or payment, it is not
    connected to this project.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-content-is-maintained">How content is maintained</h2>
  <p>
    Guides are written for this site and checked against current official documentation before
    publication. The process is described in the <a href="/how-it-works/">how it works page</a> and
    the <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>. Publication and update dates come from
    the article metadata. We do not use invented author biographies, customer testimonials, search
    volume figures, or success rates.
  </p>
  <p>
    Software menus and device settings change. When a guide is reported as outdated, it is checked
    and either corrected or marked for revision. The
    <a href="/corrections-policy/">corrections policy</a> explains what information helps and what
    happens next. We cannot guarantee a reply time, and we cannot diagnose an individual device by
    email.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-the-site-is-funded">How the site is funded</h2>
  <p>
    At the time of publication, this site carries no advertising, sponsored placements, or
    affiliate links. If that changes, the change will be disclosed on the
    <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure page</a> and in the affected content
    before any link is used. Product recommendations are not currently a source of revenue.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site does not sell visitor data. Contact messages are used to answer or investigate the
    message, and anonymous guide feedback is used to prioritise updates. The
    <a href="/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a> sets out the details, including the third-party
    services that may be used to deliver a contact message.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Use the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> for corrections, accessibility problems, privacy
    questions, or feedback on a guide. Include the guide URL and the device or software version
    where relevant. For a device that will not start, the fastest route is usually the matching
    guide rather than a message, because the guide can walk you through the checks in order.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you need manufacturer support, a repair shop, data recovery, or account recovery, the
    <a href="/get-help/">get help page</a> lists the types of support available and the information
    to prepare before you contact them.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
